::MCR_U03_L04_GP_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L04_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.5
Question:
A scatterplot rises from left to right. What association is shown?
{
=A. positive association#Correct. As x increases, y tends to increase.
~B. negative association#A negative association falls from left to right.
~C. no association#A clear rising pattern is an association.
~D. outlier only#The question describes the overall pattern, not one point.
}
::MCR_U03_L04_GP_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L04_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.6
Question:
In score \= 60 + 4h, what does the slope 4 mean?
{
~A. The starting score is 4.#The starting score is the intercept, 60.
=B. Each additional hour predicts 4 more score points.#Correct. Slope is the predicted change in score for each 1-hour increase.
~C. The score decreases by 4.#A positive slope predicts an increase.
~D. The residual is 4.#Residual compares actual and predicted values.
}
::MCR_U03_L04_GP_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L04_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.5
Question:
Which data are bivariate numerical data?
{
~A. favorite subject only#One categorical variable is not bivariate numerical data.
~B. grade level and club name#These are categorical labels, not two numerical variables.
=C. hours studied and test score for each student#Correct. Both variables are numerical and paired for each student.
~D. student names only#Names are labels, not numerical pairs.
}
::MCR_U03_L04_GP_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L04_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.6
Question:
A model predicts 90, but the actual value is 86. What is the residual using actual - predicted?
{
~A. 4#This reverses the subtraction order.
~B. 86#This is the actual value, not the residual.
~C. 90#This is the predicted value, not the residual.
=D. -4#Correct. Compute actual - predicted: 86 - 90 \= -4.
}
::MCR_U03_L04_GP_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L04_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.5
Question:
Why should a line of fit not be treated as perfect?
{
~A. It always has no slope.#Most lines of fit have a slope.
=B. It models a pattern but usually does not pass through every point.#Correct. A line of fit is an estimate of the trend, not an exact rule for every data value.
~C. It can only be used for categories.#Lines of fit are used with bivariate numerical data.
~D. It removes all residuals.#Residuals show that predictions can differ from actual values.
}
